Materialize is a CSS framework — it gives you pre-styled components so you don't have to write CSS from scratch. You just add class names to your HTML and it automatically makes things look good.

The Two Lines That Load It
html<!-- Icons (like the delete bin, thumbs up, etc.) -->
<link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/icon?family=Material+Icons" rel="stylesheet">

<!-- The actual Materialize styles -->
<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/materialize/1.0.0/css/materialize.min.css">

<!-- At the bottom of body — makes interactive stuff work (dropdowns, etc.) -->
<script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/materialize/1.0.0/js/materialize.min.js"></script>
<script>M.AutoInit();</script>
Without these lines, none of the Materialize classes do anything.

The Classes You Actually Use in the Exam
Cards — wrapping content in a box
html<div class="card">
  <div class="card-content">
    Your content here
  </div>
</div>

<!-- Horizontal card (image on the side) -->
<div class="card horizontal">
  <div class="card-image">
    <img src="...">
  </div>
  <div class="card-stacked">
    <div class="card-content">text here</div>
  </div>
</div>
Buttons
html<a href="..." class="btn purple waves-effect">Big button</a>
<a href="..." class="btn-small purple waves-effect">Small button</a>
<a href="..." class="btn-small red waves-effect">Red button</a>
<a href="..." class="btn-small green waves-effect">Green button</a>
Collection list (for comments, reviews, etc.)
html<ul class="collection">
  <li class="collection-item">Item one</li>
  <li class="collection-item">Item two</li>
</ul>
Input field (Materialize styled)
html<div class="input-field">
  <input type="text" id="my_input" name="text">
  <label for="my_input">Write something...</label>
  <!-- id= and for= must match exactly or the label floats wrong -->
</div>
Icons
html<i class="material-icons">delete</i>      <!-- bin icon -->
<i class="material-icons">thumb_up</i>    <!-- like -->
<i class="material-icons">thumb_down</i>  <!-- dislike -->
<i class="material-icons">search</i>      <!-- search -->
<i class="material-icons left">thumb_up</i>  <!-- icon inside a button, left aligned -->
Colour classes
htmlclass="purple"        <!-- purple background -->
class="red"           <!-- red background -->
class="green"         <!-- green background -->
class="grey-text"     <!-- grey text -->
class="red-text"      <!-- red text (used for delete links) -->
class="blue lighten-5" <!-- light blue background (used for flash messages) -->
Grid system (dividing the page into columns)
html<!-- Page is divided into 12 columns -->
<div class="col s4">   <!-- takes up 4/12 = 33% on small screens -->
<div class="col s8">   <!-- takes up 8/12 = 67% -->
<div class="col s12">  <!-- takes up full width -->
<!-- s = small screen, m = medium, l = large -->
Card panel (flash message box)
html<div class="card-panel blue lighten-5">
  Some message here
</div>

The Things That Catch People Out
waves-effect — always add this to buttons, it gives the ripple animation. Without it the button still works but looks plain.
id= and for= on inputs must match — if they don't, clicking the label won't focus the input and Materialize's floating label animation breaks.
browser-default on a select — Materialize hides native selects and replaces them with its own. In an exam just add class="browser-default" to keep the normal browser dropdown, it's much simpler.
html<!-- Use this in exams — simple and reliable -->
<select name="book_id" class="browser-default">
  <option value="">Choose...</option>
  {% for book in books %}
    <option value="{{ book.id }}">{{ book.title }}</option>
  {% endfor %}
</select>
M.AutoInit() at the bottom — this activates all Materialize components. If you forget it, dropdowns and other interactive components won't work.

The Pattern You'll Write Most in the Exam
Almost every panel follows this exact structure:
html<div class="panel" style="width: 30%;">
  <h6 style="padding:8px 12px; margin:0; font-weight:bold;">
    Heading
  </h6>
  <div class="scroll-area">
    {% for item in items %}
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-content">
          <p><strong>{{ item.name }}</strong></p>
          <a href="/item/{{ item.id }}" class="btn-small purple waves-effect">View</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    {% endfor %}
  </div>
</div>
